.TH CCHAN_TIMED_SEND 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cchan_timed_send \- send a message with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t cchan_timed_send(cchan *ch,
                               cmessage_t *msg,
                               uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Works like cchan_send(), but waits for free space for at most
timeout_us microseconds. The queue is the one that the channel picks
for the calling thread.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch          Channel to send through
msg         Message to send. On success the ownership of the data moves to
            the channel.
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0
            does not wait, which makes the call cchan_try_send.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - The message was sent
ccol_invalid_args         - ch or msg is NULL, msg->data is NULL
                            while msg->size is not 0, or msg->data
                            is not NULL while msg->size is 0
ccol_not_permitted        - Sends on the chosen direction are turned off
ccol_container_full       - The timeout is 0 and the chosen queue is
                            full
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call never blocks for longer than the timeout. The direction is
decided from the ID of the calling thread.

With a timeout of 0 the call doesn't wait at all. It does exactly what
cchan_try_send does and returns that result, so a full queue
gives ccol_container_full and not ccol_timed_out.

A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline the clock can hold, which in practice means
waiting with no end. It never wraps around into the past.

The timeout is measured on CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Changes to the system's
wall clock (made by an administrator, by NTP, or by resuming a virtual
machine) don't make it longer or shorter, and the call waits for the
duration that the caller gave.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cchan_send (3),
.BR cchan_try_send (3)
